wasm32: __clone must run the child through clone_entry so it gets its own stack

The port's __clone() builds a clone_entry trampoline whose only job is to
`global.set __stack_pointer` to the new thread's stack, but then passes the
ORIGINAL func/arg to SYS_clone: the trampoline is dead code, the malloc'd
args leak, and every pthread runs on the parent's shadow stack (each thread
worker's wasm instance starts with the module's default __stack_pointer).
Two threads that both park in pthread_cond_wait then have their on-stack
`struct waiter` nodes 32 bytes apart on the same stack and corrupt musl's
condvar waiter list — the "cond ping-pong wedges at round 2" bug that took
down CPython threading and the ffmpeg 7 CLI on the wasm kernel.

--- a/src/thread/wasm32/clone.c
+++ b/src/thread/wasm32/clone.c
@@ -42,6 +42,8 @@ int __clone(int (*func)(void *), void *stack, int flags, void *arg, ...) {
 
   struct clone_entry_arg *args = malloc(sizeof(*args));
   args->stack = stack;
+  args->func = func;
+  args->arg = arg;
 
-  return __syscall(SYS_clone, func, arg, flags, parent_tid, child_tid, tls);
+  return __syscall(SYS_clone, clone_entry, args, flags, parent_tid, child_tid, tls);
 }
